PROTECTED BASELINE:
- Every accepted RangeWater 1.2.0 record, calculation, map interaction, help behavior,
  precision-loupe correction, backup path, and production identity remains protected.
- Existing one-line paddock plans must migrate forward without being redrawn.
- No accepted permanent pasture boundary is silently split, renamed, or replaced.

🪨 BLOCK 2 — PROPOSED PRODUCT SCOPE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The proposed RangeWater 1.3.0 scope contains two principal capabilities:

1. Multiple Polystrand Planning
   - more than one temporary divider in one mapped pasture;
   - independent temporary-divider plans in multiple pastures;
   - straight assisted placement and custom waypoint routing;
   - combined-region acreage feedback; and
   - preservation of the future no-redraw permanent-conversion seam.

2. Field Ledger
   - seven fast record categories adapted from the inspected Track & Trace FARM flow;
   - precise map-point or foreground device-location capture;
   - optional pasture association;
   - map markers and a filterable newest-first ledger;
   - task completion plus individual edit and archive controls; and
   - complete offline Room and .rangewater backup coverage.

The two capabilities share RangeWater's map and backup system but do not silently alter
one another. A Field Ledger record does not create a divider, and a divider does not
create a Field Ledger record.

🎮 BLOCK 3 — MULTIPLE POLYSTRAND PRODUCT MEANING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- User-facing feature name: Plan Polystrand.
- A polystrand divider is a saved planning line representing proposed temporary fence
  inside one mapped pasture.
- A plan may contain one or several dividers.
- A divider may be straight or may contain ordered interior waypoints.
- Waypoints allow practical routing around strip pits, cliffs, trees, buildings, roads,
  ponds, fixed water infrastructure, and other obstacles.
- The line is not a surveyed boundary, permanent fence, gate, movement route, circuit,
  herd location, or proof that temporary fence has been installed.
- The parent pasture remains one canonical PastureEntity.
- Multiple polystrand plans are allowed across the ranch. Planning in one pasture never
  consumes a ranch-wide or pasture-wide one-time allowance.
- RangeWater 1.3.0 does not permanently convert a divider into child pastures.

🎮 BLOCK 4 — POLYSTRAND PLACEMENT MODES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4A. ASSISTED EQUAL-AREA MODE

- The operator chooses an intended region count or an equal-area objective supported by
  the accepted implementation.
- RangeWater may suggest a valid straight boundary-to-boundary divider near the
  requested acreage balance.
- Suggested geometry is a planning aid, not a surveyed or guaranteed equal division.
- The operator reviews both the line and displayed acreage before Save.
- If terrain or obstacles require a bend, the operator may switch to Custom Waypoints.

4B. CUSTOM WAYPOINT MODE

- For the first divider, the operator places a start endpoint on the selected pasture boundary.
- The operator may place zero or more ordered interior waypoints.
- The first divider ends on another valid part of the boundary.
- Later dividers may begin or end at a boundary node or a deliberately selected shared
  interior polystrand node when the complete network still forms valid regions.
- The precision loupe is available for endpoints and every waypoint.
- Waypoints must remain inside or on the selected parent pasture.
- The line may not self-cross, double back along the boundary, overlap another active
  divider segment, or create zero-area or otherwise invalid regions.

4C. EDITING CONTROLS

- Add Waypoint places a new point on a selected divider segment.
- Move Point repositions an endpoint or interior waypoint with the precision loupe.
- Remove Waypoint removes only an interior point; endpoints cannot be removed separately.
- Undo reverses the latest in-memory placement or movement.
- Cancel restores the last saved plan without a database write.
- Save remains unavailable until the complete combined plan validates.
- A divider may be renamed, archived, reactivated when safe, or deleted with confirmation.

🎮 BLOCK 5 — COMBINED GEOMETRY AND ACREAGE FEEDBACK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- All active dividers in one plan are evaluated together against the parent polygon.
- The combined linework must polygonize the parent into two or more valid, positive-area,
  non-overlapping planning regions.
- Every derived region remains contained by the parent pasture.
- Region interiors may not overlap, and their union must reconcile with the parent.
- Turf remains authoritative for displayed geodesic acreage.
- Derived region acreages must reconcile to parent mapped acreage within 0.5% numerical
  tolerance, preserving RangeWater's accepted map-estimate semantics.
- During placement, RangeWater displays the current valid region acreages and updates
  them as endpoints or waypoints move.
- The interface may offer an equal-area target, but the operator remains free to accept
  unequal acreages.
- Default region labels are generated in stable plan order and may be renamed after the
  combined geometry is valid.
- Any geometry edit that changes region topology requires truthful label review rather
  than silently attaching an old label to an unrelated new region.
- A small numerical implementation safety threshold may reject sliver regions; the UI
  must not describe that threshold as a universal agricultural minimum.

🎮 BLOCK 6 — MULTI-DIVIDER OPERATOR WORKFLOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Select a pasture.
2. Choose Plan Polystrand.
3. Open an existing active plan or create a new plan.
4. Choose Assisted Equal Area or Custom Waypoints.
5. Place the first endpoint, optional waypoints, and final endpoint.
6. Review all derived regions and their mapped acreage estimates.
7. Save the divider or continue adding another divider.
8. Review the complete plan, names, linework, and region acreages.
9. Save or Cancel.

Rules:
- A pasture may have one active planning set containing multiple active dividers.
- Archived historical planning sets may coexist with the active set.
- A divider in one pasture has no effect on any other pasture.
- Quartering, strip-grazing layouts, wagon-wheel-like arrangements, and obstacle-aware
  routes are allowed when the combined geometry passes validation.
- Dividers may deliberately meet at a shared interior polystrand node, allowing validated
  wagon-wheel-like layouts.
- A line crossing that does not reference the same durable shared node is rejected.
- A visual crossing alone never creates a junction, and an interior polystrand node is
  never confused with a permanent FenceJunctionEntity.

🪨 BLOCK 7 — PROPOSED POLYSTRAND DATA MODEL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7A. PLAN CONTAINER

Table: paddock_plans
Entity: PaddockPlanEntity

Fields:
- id: Long, stable primary key
- pastureId: Long, required, indexed
- name: String, required, trimmed
- createdAt: Long, required
- updatedAt: Long, required
- archivedAt: Long?, nullable

Rules:
- At most one active plan container exists per pasture.
- Archived plans remain available for history and possible future reuse.
- The parent pasture uses ON DELETE RESTRICT / NO ACTION.

7B. POLYSTRAND NODES

Table: paddock_plan_nodes
Entity: PaddockPlanNodeEntity

Fields:
- id: Long, stable primary key
- planId: Long, required, indexed
- nodeKind: PaddockPlanNodeKind, required
- boundaryJunctionAId: Long?, nullable
- boundaryJunctionBId: Long?, nullable
- boundarySegmentRatio: Double?, nullable
- latitude: Double?, nullable
- longitude: Double?, nullable
- createdAt: Long, required
- updatedAt: Long, required

PaddockPlanNodeKind enum:
- BOUNDARY_ANCHOR
- INTERIOR_JUNCTION
- INTERIOR_WAYPOINT

Rules:
- BOUNDARY_ANCHOR requires one canonical consecutive junction pair and one finite ratio
  from 0.0 through 1.0; its coordinate is derived from the current canonical boundary.
- INTERIOR_JUNCTION and INTERIOR_WAYPOINT require finite latitude and longitude inside
  the parent pasture and must not carry boundary-anchor fields.
- Multiple divider paths may reference one INTERIOR_JUNCTION to create an intentional
  wagon-wheel center or other shared temporary-fence meeting point.
- An INTERIOR_WAYPOINT normally belongs to one divider path and bends that route without
  creating a connection to another divider.
- Polystrand nodes are planning geometry and never become permanent FenceJunctionEntity
  rows merely because they are saved or shared.
- Sharing is explicit. Placing one line visually over another node does not silently
  convert that node into an INTERIOR_JUNCTION or attach the lines.
- Moving an INTERIOR_JUNCTION previews every active divider that references it and every
  affected derived region. Save revalidates and commits the complete affected plan
  atomically; failure leaves the node and every divider unchanged.
- A shared node cannot be deleted while any active or archived divider path still
  references it. The operator must first remove or redirect each reference, or delete
  the referencing divider under its own confirmation rules.
- Archiving one divider never deletes a shared node used by another divider. Archived
  dividers retain their node references so their historical geometry remains recoverable.
- Deleting a divider removes only that divider's ordered references. A node may be
  deleted as orphan cleanup only when no active or archived divider references it.
- Removing, archiving, reactivating, or deleting a divider must leave the remaining
  active network valid. If it would leave an invalid active plan, the operation is
  blocked unless the operator archives the complete plan in the same transaction.
- Changing a node between INTERIOR_WAYPOINT and INTERIOR_JUNCTION is an explicit guarded
  operation, not an incidental result of dragging or line overlap.
- Promoting a private waypoint to a shared junction requires an explicit Connect/Share
  action, selection of the divider path being connected, and atomic validation of the
  complete resulting plan before the new reference and node kind are committed.
- Demoting a shared junction to a private waypoint is blocked while more than one divider
  references it. After the operator explicitly unlinks all additional dividers, demotion
  may proceed only if the remaining divider and complete active plan still validate.
- Failed promotion, connection, unlink, or demotion leaves node kind, references, divider
  geometry, region labels, and acreages unchanged.

7C. DIVIDERS

Table: paddock_dividers
Entity: PaddockDividerEntity

Fields:
- id: Long, stable primary key
- planId: Long, required, indexed
- name: String, required, trimmed
- sequence: Int, required, zero-based within the plan
- createdAt: Long, required
- updatedAt: Long, required
- archivedAt: Long?, nullable

Rules:
- Divider sequence is stable and contiguous after saved reorder.
- A plan must contain at least one active divider before it is displayed as active.

7D. ORDERED DIVIDER PATHS

Table: paddock_divider_node_refs
Entity: PaddockDividerNodeRefEntity

Fields:
- dividerId: Long, part of composite primary key
- sequence: Int, part of composite primary key, zero-based
- nodeId: Long, required, indexed

Rules:
- Node-reference sequence is contiguous after every save and contains at least two nodes.
- Consecutive references resolve the persisted polyline segments.
- A divider may reference boundary anchors, private waypoints, and deliberate shared
  interior junctions in a validated order.
- No divider may reference the same node twice unless a separately ratified closed-loop
  behavior is added later.
- Coordinate truth is geographic or canonical-boundary anchored, never screen pixels.
- Node, reference, divider, and plan changes commit atomically.

7E. REGION LABELS

- Derived region geometry and acreage are not persisted as duplicate polygons or totals.
- User labels may be persisted only with a deterministic region identity derived from
  the validated parent-and-divider topology.
- If a topology edit invalidates that identity, Save requires label review.
- Implementation may not guess that the largest, nearest, or first polygon is the same
  historical region without a tested deterministic rule.

7F. MIGRATION OF ACCEPTED 1.2.0 PLANS

- Every existing paddock_split_plans row becomes one active or archived PaddockPlanEntity
  with two boundary-anchor PaddockPlanNodeEntity rows, one PaddockDividerEntity, and two
  ordered PaddockDividerNodeRefEntity rows.
- Existing stable plan ID, pasture relationship, name, boundary anchors, endpoint ratios,
  side labels, createdAt, updatedAt, and archivedAt must be preserved or mapped through an
  explicit documented compatibility record.
- Migration may not require the operator to redraw the accepted line.
- After migration, the original straight divider must render at the same coordinates and
  produce the same two acreage results within accepted tolerance.

🎮 BLOCK 8 — FIELD LEDGER RECORD TYPES AND MEANING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
User-facing entry point: Field Log
User-facing history: Field Ledger

Record types:
- Task
- Observation
- Input
- Repair
- Animal
- Weather
- Note

Meanings:
- Task: work that should be completed and may carry Open, Completed, or Cancelled state.
- Observation: a general field condition or finding.
- Input: an applied material, feed, seed, fertilizer, treatment, or other operator note;
  it is not automatically a regulated inventory or veterinary record.
- Repair: damage, maintenance need, or completed repair.
- Animal: a location-bound livestock or wildlife observation; it does not modify HerdEntity.
- Weather: a local condition observed by the operator; it is not an official weather feed.
- Note: any other concise location-bound field record.

🎮 BLOCK 9 — FIELD LOG CAPTURE WORKFLOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. From the map overflow or a selected map point, choose Field Log.
2. Choose the record type.
3. Confirm or move the map point with the accepted precision loupe.
4. Optionally choose Use My Location while RangeWater is in the foreground.
5. Enter the required note and review the automatically associated pasture, if any.
6. For Task, leave the default status Open or choose another valid status.
7. Save or Cancel.

Location rules:
- The saved latitude/longitude is the crosshair coordinate shown to the operator.
- A foreground device-location choice requires ordinary Android permission and displays
  reported accuracy when available before Save.
- Denying location permission does not block manual map-point logging.
- No background location, tracking, route history, geofence, or continuous GPS is added.
- If the point lies inside exactly one mapped pasture, RangeWater suggests that pasture.
- If boundaries overlap or no pasture contains the point, the user may choose a pasture
  or save the record with no pasture association.
- Moving a pasture boundary later does not rewrite the historical recorded coordinate.

🪨 BLOCK 10 — PROPOSED FIELD LEDGER DATA MODEL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Table: field_records
Entity: FieldRecordEntity

Fields:
- id: Long, auto-generated stable primary key
- recordType: FieldRecordType, required
- note: String, required, trimmed, non-blank
- latitude: Double, required, finite
- longitude: Double, required, finite
- pastureId: Long?, nullable, indexed
- taskStatus: FieldTaskStatus?, nullable
- observedAt: Long, required
- createdAt: Long, required
- updatedAt: Long, required
- completedAt: Long?, nullable
- archivedAt: Long?, nullable

FieldRecordType enum:
- TASK
- OBSERVATION
- INPUT
- REPAIR
- ANIMAL
- WEATHER
- NOTE

FieldTaskStatus enum:
- OPEN
- COMPLETED
- CANCELLED

Rules:
- taskStatus is required only for TASK and must be null for every other type.
- completedAt is required only when a Task is Completed.
- pastureId uses ON DELETE SET NULL so deleting an otherwise-unblocked pasture does not
  erase the historical field record or its coordinate.
- Editing a record preserves its ID and createdAt and advances updatedAt.
- Archive is the normal removal action. Permanent deletion, if exposed, requires a
  separate warning and may not be a ledger-wide Clear command.
- The inspected Track & Trace prototype's destructive Clear All behavior is not copied.

🎮 BLOCK 11 — FIELD LEDGER AND MAP PRESENTATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Field records appear in a toggleable Field Records map layer.
- Marker color or icon distinguishes the seven record types without relying on color alone.
- Selecting a marker opens its type, note, time, pasture association, task state, and
  Edit, Complete/Reopen where applicable, Archive, and Close actions.
- The Field Ledger orders active records newest observedAt first.
- Filters include record type, pasture, Task status, and active/archived state.
- A text search may match notes and displayed pasture names without requiring network access.
- Selecting a ledger record may focus the map on its saved coordinate without changing
  the durable camera preference until the ordinary camera-saving rules apply.
- Empty states explain whether there are no records or whether active filters hide them.
- Field Ledger markers remain distinct from herds, water points, gates, junctions, and
  polystrand geometry on bright and dark imagery.

🔧 BLOCK 12 — ROOM MIGRATION AND TRANSACTION CONTRACT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Advance RangeWaterDatabase from version 8 to version 9.
- Add explicit MIGRATION_8_9 after every accepted prior migration.
- Create the normalized polystrand plan/node/divider/path structures and field_records.
- Migrate existing paddock_split_plans under Block 7F before removing or superseding any
  legacy table structure.
- Preserve every schema-8 pasture, junction, vertex, water, assignment, gate, herd,
  movement, circuit, role, forage observation, and accepted relationship unchanged.
- Destructive migration and fallbackToDestructiveMigration remain prohibited.
- Plan saves, divider reorder, node/path replacement, region-label reconciliation, Task
  completion, and archive/reactivation operations must be transactional.
- Shared-node move, link, unlink, divider archive/reactivation, divider deletion, and
  orphan cleanup must validate all affected references and combined geometry in one
  transaction.
- Waypoint-to-junction promotion and junction-to-waypoint demotion must update node kind
  and ordered references atomically; a failed guard or geometry check rolls back both.
- Node-reference foreign keys use RESTRICT / NO ACTION for node deletion; cascade may be
  used only from a deliberately deleted divider to its own ordered reference rows.
- UI validation does not replace DAO-owned validation.

🔧 BLOCK 13 — ARCHIVE FORMAT 4
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- RangeWater 1.3.0 writes .rangewater archive format 4.
- Format 4 includes paddock plan containers, nodes, dividers, ordered node references,
  preserved region labels,
  and Field Ledger records with exact version-specific record counts.
- The reader accepts formats 1, 2, 3, and 4.
- Formats 1 and 2 upgrade with empty polystrand and Field Ledger collections.
- Format 3 migrates its accepted single-line paddock plans using the same semantic mapping
  as MIGRATION_8_9 and adds an empty Field Ledger collection.
- Unsupported newer formats, invalid coordinates, broken anchors, missing parents,
  malformed node-reference sequences, invalid task states, and unreconciled geometry are
  rejected before live replacement.
- Snapshot, replace-all restore, rollback archives, and Undo Last Restore include every
  new schema-9 record.
- Restore remains checksummed, validated before writing, foreign-key ordered, and atomic.

🔧 BLOCK 14 — AUTOMATED ACCEPTANCE GATES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
JVM / GEOMETRY TESTS:
1. Straight legacy dividers resolve identically after semantic migration.
2. A valid waypoint divider routes around an obstacle-shaped test area and remains inside.
3. Self-crossing, boundary-overlap, outside-pasture, zero-area, and overlapping-divider
   geometry is rejected.
4. Multiple valid dividers produce the expected region count.
5. Several dividers may meet at one explicit interior shared node, while an unmodeled
   visual crossing is rejected.
6. Region interiors are disjoint and exhaustive within 0.5% acreage tolerance.
7. Equal-area suggestions are deterministic and disclose approximation.
8. Boundary anchors survive valid canonical junction movement.
9. Cancel and interrupted precision placement commit no change.

ANDROID ROOM TESTS:
1. MIGRATION_8_9 preserves a populated real schema-8 database.
2. Every existing 1.2.0 split becomes an equivalent one-divider plan without redraw.
3. Multiple dividers, waypoints, and shared interior nodes survive close and reopen in
   multiple pastures.
4. Invalid combined plans and partial node/path writes roll back completely.
5. Moving one shared interior node revalidates every referencing active divider and
   either commits the complete valid plan or rolls back every affected row.
6. A referenced shared node cannot be deleted, and deleting one divider does not remove
   a node still referenced by another active or archived divider.
7. Orphan cleanup removes a node only after its final divider reference is deliberately
   deleted; archive alone does not orphan or remove historical geometry.
8. Archiving, reactivating, or deleting a divider cannot leave an invalid active network.
9. Drawing across or dragging onto a private waypoint does not silently promote or share it.
10. Explicit waypoint-to-junction promotion creates the intended additional reference
    only when the complete plan validates, and invalid promotion rolls back completely.
11. Junction-to-waypoint demotion is blocked while multiple dividers reference the node;
    after explicit unlinking, a valid demotion commits atomically.
12. Field records of all seven types survive close and reopen.
13. Task completion/reopen timestamps and status rules are enforced.
14. Pasture deletion sets a FieldRecord pastureId to null without deleting the record.
15. Archive/reactivation and filtered observations remain consistent.

ARCHIVE TESTS:
1. Formats 1 and 2 restore every legacy record with empty new collections.
2. Format 3 restores its straight plan at identical coordinates and acreage.
3. Format 4 round-trips multiple dividers, nodes, ordered paths, labels, Field Ledger records,
   Task states, IDs, coordinates, timestamps, and archive state.
4. Malformed geometry or relationships are rejected before replacement.
5. Undo Last Restore returns the complete former schema-9 state.

BUILD GATES:
- Imagery-gateway regression suite passes.
- JVM tests pass.
- Android Room instrumentation tests pass.
- Debug APK builds.
- Stable field APK builds and its certificate is verified.
- Play AAB builds, signs, and matches the configured upload certificate.
- No private signing material is committed.

🎮 BLOCK 15 — PHYSICAL-GLASS ACCEPTANCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Required devices:
- Michael's current Samsung phone
- Samsung Tab S7

Required sequence:
1. Upgrade an existing RangeWater 1.2.0 installation containing a saved straight plan.
2. Confirm every accepted ranch record, preference, help behavior, and calculation survives.
3. Confirm the former straight plan appears unchanged and can accept another divider.
4. Create multiple dividers in one pasture and at least one divider in another pasture.
5. Quarter a pasture or create another valid three-or-more-region layout.
6. Route a waypoint divider around a real or representative obstacle.
7. Exercise endpoint and waypoint Add, Move, Remove, Undo, Cancel, and Save.
8. Confirm live region acreages reconcile to the parent and remain after cold reopen.
9. Confirm one pasture's plan never removes or disables another pasture's plan.
10. Create a shared interior junction used by several dividers; move it and confirm every
    referencing line and region previews and saves together.
11. Attempt to delete the referenced junction and confirm the operation is blocked.
12. Archive and delete one referencing divider; confirm other divider geometry survives
    and the shared node remains until its final reference is deliberately removed.
13. Draw or drag another divider across a private waypoint and confirm no silent sharing.
14. Use the explicit Connect/Share action to promote that waypoint to a shared junction;
    confirm the intended divider joins and the complete plan remains valid.
15. Attempt to demote the still-shared junction and confirm the operation is blocked;
    explicitly unlink the additional divider, then confirm valid demotion can be saved.
16. Create each of the seven Field Ledger record types at precise map points.
17. Create a Task, complete it, reopen it, edit it, and archive it.
18. Verify manual map placement works with device location permission denied.
19. If foreground location is exercised, verify the displayed accuracy and saved point.
20. Filter the ledger by type, pasture, status, and archive state; select a result on map.
21. Edit a pasture boundary and confirm historical field coordinates do not move.
22. Create format-4 backup, restore on the other device, and verify plans and records.
23. Restore a known format-3 backup and confirm the old plan migrates without redraw.
24. Use Undo Last Restore and confirm the complete prior state returns.

Acceptance authority:
- CI proves only automated contracts and artifact identity.
- Michael's physical-glass report is required before field acceptance.
- A controlled Google Play 1.2.0-to-1.3.0 in-place migration must preserve real ranch
  records before production promotion may be considered.
- Production publication requires a separate explicit instruction from Michael for the
  exact internally tested versionCode 21 bundle. No rebuild may inherit that authority.

🖍️ BLOCK 16 — HUMAN-EDITABLE WORDING AND VISUAL TUNING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The following may be tuned during review or physical acceptance without changing data meaning:
- `Field Log` versus `Log Field Record` command wording;
- concise record-type descriptions and empty-state text;
- divider, waypoint, planning-fill, and ledger-marker colors and iconography;
- marker clustering threshold and phone-safe ledger spacing;
- precision-loupe size and offset under the already accepted coordinate-truth rule;
- equal-area disclosure wording; and
- default generated plan, divider, and region labels.

The following are not mere tuning changes:
- permanent conversion into new PastureEntity records;
- allowing invalid intersections or exterior waypoints;
- automatic changes to herds, movements, circuits, gates, water, rest, or forage;
- background location or tracking;
- changing Room or archive versions; or
- cloud synchronization or multi-user assignment.

🪨 BLOCK 17 — EXPLICITLY OUT OF SCOPE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- permanent conversion of temporary dividers into child pastures
- archiving the parent pasture and creating permanent child histories
- automatic permanent-gate redistribution during a future conversion
- parcel ownership lookup or SageWire Parcel Service integration
- cloud accounts, synchronization, shared assignments, or notifications
- photos, videos, or other Field Ledger attachments
- regulated veterinary-treatment, chemical-application, or accounting compliance claims
- automatic weather-service ingestion
- divider material, reel, post, energizer, labor, or cost estimation
- terrain, slope, land-cover, pipe-pressure, or hydraulic analysis
- automatic stocking rate, rotation dates, carrying capacity, or biological readiness
- background GPS, employee tracking, or route recording
- iPhone or iPad implementation

These remain separate proposals and require their own ratified authority.

